EXT. DESERT SALT FLAT - DAY

Glare. White crust to every horizon. A jeep sits with its hood up, wheels sunk to the axles.

MARIBEL SOTO (30s, wide-brim hat, cracked lips) kneels at the engine. Her brother ANDRÉS (20s, sunburned neck) sits in the shade of the jeep, rationing a water bottle with one eye.

She wipes her hands on her jeans. He caps the bottle.

ANDRÉS
Anything?

MARIBEL
Radiator's dry.

ANDRÉS
How dry?

MARIBEL
Bone.

ANDRÉS
How much water do we have?

MARIBEL
You're holding it.

He turns the bottle upside down. A few drops.

ANDRÉS
I thought we had four.

MARIBEL
We had four. You drank two on the first day.

ANDRÉS
You told me to.

MARIBEL
I told you to sip.

ANDRÉS
I sipped.

MARIBEL
Big sips.

ANDRÉS
You said the road was forty miles.

MARIBEL
It was forty miles.

ANDRÉS
It's been eighty.

MARIBEL
I know.

ANDRÉS
We took the wrong turn.

MARIBEL
You took the wrong turn.

ANDRÉS
I was reading your map.

MARIBEL
My map was fine.

ANDRÉS
Your map was upside down.

She stands. Wipes her face. He rises with her, squinting.

On the horizon: a shimmer. A dot. A dust plume.

They both freeze.

ANDRÉS (CONT'D)
Is that a car?

MARIBEL
Don't hope.

The dot grows. A battered pickup rolls out of the glare and stops thirty feet away.

The door opens. OLD MAN BENEDICTO (70s, straw hat, a jug of water slung over one shoulder) climbs down. He looks at the jeep. Looks at them.

BENEDICTO
You are very far from the road.

Maribel steps forward.

MARIBEL
Please. Water.

He unslings the jug. Holds it out. She takes it with both hands and drinks. Andrés watches, trembling.

BENEDICTO
Slowly. Slowly.
